From: kakaotalk-on-wine
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: Draw an astral character as one character

Emoji come out as a pair of empty boxes. Three things have to be wrong at
once for that, and they are, so fixing any one of them alone changes nothing
-- which is why a long search through font substitutions, replacements and
link keys never moved it.

ExtTextOutW takes UTF-16 and win32u walks it one code unit at a time, so
U+1F33E is looked up as U+D83C and then as U+DF3E. Neither is in any font's
cmap and both come back as the missing glyph. Measured, drawing a message in
KakaoTalk under WINEDEBUG=+font:

    NtGdiExtTextOutW 0xa410226, 120, 267, 00001004,
        (120,267)-(154,288), L"\d83c\df3e", 2, (nil))

No ETO_GLYPH_INDEX, so these are characters; no dx, so the advances come
from the glyph metrics. The loops that split the string are the ones that
have to stop splitting it in the middle of a character: render_string draws,
get_total_extents and the nulldrv path measure and draw, and
font_GetTextExtentExPoint measures. A pair is one character, so it is
measured once; both of its code units still get an entry in dxs, because the
caller sized that array by the length of the string.

Composing it is not enough on its own. NtGdiGetGlyphOutline masks its
character with 0xffff before passing it on, so a composed U+1F33E arrives as
U+F33E -- which no font has either. The mask is worth keeping for a caller
that passes a WCHAR with rubbish in the high half; it is not worth applying
to a codepoint that is plainly astral. This is the step bug 53929 does not
mention, and is where a fix that only composes the pair still fails.

And the glyph cache is indexed by the character and paged over a table sized
for 0x10000, so an astral codepoint indexes past the end of it: U+1F33E asks
for page 499 of 256. Sizing the table for the whole of Unicode costs
pointers, not glyphs -- the pages themselves are still allocated on first
use.

Nothing below the walk needed changing. get_glyph_index passes its UINT
through to FT_Get_Char_Index, which takes an FT_ULong, and a cmap format 12
answers for the astral planes.

ETO_GLYPH_INDEX is left alone throughout: there the array holds glyph
numbers, and one in the surrogate range is a glyph number that merely looks
like one.

Tested with a program that asks for the outline and reports its size, so the
answer is a number rather than a look at a window. Before, U+1F33E came back
as 88 bytes in a 13x22 box, the notdef; after, 3144 bytes in a 37x33 box, and
the same glyph whether the emoji font is selected directly or reached through
a font link. Colour emoji stay monochrome -- win32u's text path has no colour
glyph support at all, which is a separate and much larger matter.

Wine bug: https://bugs.winehq.org/show_bug.cgi?id=53929
---
--- a/dlls/win32u/ntgdi_private.h
+++ b/dlls/win32u/ntgdi_private.h
@@ -645,4 +645,33 @@
 
 void set_gdi_client_ptr( HGDIOBJ handle, void *ptr );
 
+
+/* Combine a surrogate pair into the character it stands for.
+ *
+ * ExtTextOutW takes UTF-16 and the loops that draw it walk one code unit at a
+ * time, so without this an astral character is looked up as each of its two
+ * halves -- U+D83C and U+DF3E rather than U+1F33E -- and neither half is in
+ * any font's cmap. Both come back as the missing glyph, which is the pair of
+ * boxes every emoji draws as, and no font link can help because the codepoint
+ * the linked font would answer for is never assembled.
+ *
+ * Returns the number of code units the character occupies, so the caller can
+ * step over both halves rather than drawing the second one again.
+ *
+ * Never under ETO_GLYPH_INDEX: there the array holds glyph numbers, and one
+ * in the surrogate range is a glyph number that merely looks like one.
+ */
+static inline UINT decode_utf16_char( const WCHAR *str, UINT count, UINT i, UINT flags, UINT *ch )
+{
+    if (!(flags & ETO_GLYPH_INDEX) &&
+        str[i] >= 0xd800 && str[i] <= 0xdbff &&
+        i + 1 < count && str[i + 1] >= 0xdc00 && str[i + 1] <= 0xdfff)
+    {
+        *ch = 0x10000 + ((str[i] - 0xd800) << 10) + (str[i + 1] - 0xdc00);
+        return 2;
+    }
+    *ch = str[i];
+    return 1;
+}
+
 #endif /* __WINE_NTGDI_PRIVATE_H */
